WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.900
Speaker 1: Why don't we go next to Alex from
San Diego.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.290
Alex, you're on the air.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.760
Speaker 6: Oh, hello.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.840
I didn't actually expect to be chosen, so
I have a I have a question for you.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:19.180
Yeah, I've long thought that the most important
issue in politics today is the impact that

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.089
money has on politics and that if you saw
that a lot of the other issues would naturally

00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:29.899
solve themselves over time as people's natural
instincts are very reasonable in general.

00:00:29.899 --> 00:00:33.010
Do you do you agree that this is the most
important issue or do you see something else

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.239
is the most important issue today?

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:40.420
Speaker 1: I believe that there is a sort
of triumvirate or a three headed monster of

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:46.940
sorts that are really the three most important
issues systemically.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.789
And you hit on one of them money.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:52.370
And politics is one aspect of this.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:59.531
Climate change is a sort of systemic and structural
issue where if cities end up underwater, it's

00:00:59.531 --> 00:01:02.520
not going to really matter what our opinion
about abortion is.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.390
So it's sort of like whatever issue you care
about, if we don't deal with climate change,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.930
we're not going to be able to to get that
fixed.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:15.770
And then thirdly would be how our elections
are run, meaning that they're mostly first

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.240
past the post and and for the presidency.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.060
It's an electoral college system.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.750
So all those three issues together, if we
could fix campaign finance money in politics,

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.750
if we could get to some kind of transferable
vote and get rid of the Electoral College,

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:37.060
and then if we could deal with climate change,
then we would sort of be set up to start dealing

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.049
with, like, OK, I care about education.

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:41.869
Here's how I'll be an activist on education.

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:44.270
You might care about, you know, trans rights.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.850
Here's how you can get involved in that.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.719
I think that you are right in identifying
one of the three major issues.

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:55.020
Speaker 6: So that's kind of my point, though,
I feel like a lot of those other issues can

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.241
in many ways be reduced down to the money
and politics issue like climate change, for

00:02:00.241 --> 00:02:07.039
example, if money and if money, if there was
some way to resolve campaign finance, then

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:13.220
over time, candidates would begin to more
accurately represent the will of the people.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.220
Hopefully, ideally.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.870
And most people want to do something about
climate change.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.970
Speaker 1: Yeah, I don't disagree in that.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:23.350
You're saying that over the long term that
we might get to some kind of equilibrium,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:28.890
but it would be a long time because even if
you dealt with money in politics, fossil fuels

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.640
are still advantaged in a number of different
ways, including by the sheer market power

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.660
that they have in ways that are not directly
connected to money in politics.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.610
So I'm with you about the long term.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:43.360
I think if we wanted to more quickly do something
about it, I would still put these three issues

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.760
sort of as the the three headed monster that
needs to be dealt with.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.500
Speaker 6: I see very and thank you for that.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.940
I have one other very minor thing if you don't.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.360
Right.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.110
The other thing is I've I've had this idea
and I don't know if anyone else has talked

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:02.570
about it and I've heard it talked about, but
it's related to like how to address worker

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:05.070
executive pay disparity.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:12.890
What an addition to also being able to increase
taxes on very wealthy people and, you know,

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:13.890
and wealth in general.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:18.520
And I realize this idea may not maybe unpassable,
but nonetheless, I want to know what you think

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:19.520
of it.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:26.300
Why not tie the tax rate for corporations
to the OP, to the difference between, like

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:30.700
the highest paid executives and the median
pay of a company, such that the bigger that

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:33.320
disparity, the more the company pays and some
additional tax.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.700
And if they want to reduce that tax, they
have to improve average worker pay.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.560
Speaker 1: It's a super interesting idea.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:46.250
And so to give an example, the way it might
work would be if you if the CEO earns a hundred

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:51.700
times more than the lowest paid person, you
pay a 12 percent corporate tax rate.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.030
But if the CEO earns a thousand times that
of the lowest paid person, your tax rate goes

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:57.750
up to 40 percent, something like that.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:58.750
Right?

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.000
I mean, not those numbers, but that's the
idea.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.480
Yeah, I think it's super interesting.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:04.130
I think there's a couple problems.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:10.220
One is I don't know how something like that
even comes close to passing, but let's let's

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:11.220
ignore that.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.220
Right.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:13.220
Because we're talking about whether it's a
good idea.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:19.509
I think you still because the corporate tax
rate, first of all, applies only to certain

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:20.509
businesses.

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:24.379
Any Self Proprietorship, LLC s corporation,
those are passed through to the corporate

00:04:24.379 --> 00:04:29.020
tax rate, doesn't even make a difference to
them unless you deal with that.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:34.000
And the fact that you can still deduct and
loophole your way down to zero tax liability,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.939
you would need to still combine it with an
alternative minimum tax for corporations.

00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:40.000
You'd have to do a bunch of other stuff.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.240
It's interesting, practically speaking, I
don't see any way that it would pass in the

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:47.210
next 20 years, but I think it's a really interesting
idea.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.560
Speaker 6: OK, thank you for that.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:50.560
